{"type": {"quote": "By generating bovine and porcine DNAJC14 knockout cells, we could show that (i) replication of 6 distinct noncp pestivirus species (A to D, F, and G) depends on DNAJC14, (ii) the pestiviral replicase NS3-5B can assemble into functional complexes in the absence of DNAJC14, and (iii) all cp pestiviruses replicate their RNA and generate infectious progeny independent of host DNAJC14. Together, these findings confirm DNAJC14 as a pivotal cellular cofactor for the replication and maintenance of the noncp biotype of pestiviruses.", "location": "abstract", "reason": "Both the shipped record and the blind coder classify this study as a replication. Using newly generated CRISPR/Cas9 DNAJC14-knockout and GST-Jiv90 rescue cell lines, the authors collect new experimental data that directly re-test and confirm the previously published finding that BVDV-1 (and other noncp pestiviruses) depend on cellular DNAJC14/Jiv90 for regulated NS2-3 cleavage and RNA replication.", "method": "argon"}, "original": {"quote": "In agreement with these functional observations, our Western blot analysis of the NS2-3 cleavage in all four MDBK cell lines confirmed the expected regulation of NS2-3 cleavage by DNAJC14 (
Fig. 6B
). The overexpression of the functional Jiv90(WT) domain in MDBK DNAJC14-KO Jiv90(WT) cells led to deregulated NS2-3 cleavage, resulting in increased NS3 release in MDBK DNAJC14-KO Jiv90(WT) cells infected with NCP7 compared to naive MDBK cells (
Fig. 6B
), leading to a characteristic steady-state level of NS2-3 cleavage in the infected MDBK DNAJC14-KO Jiv90(WT) rescue cells. Furthermore, higher steady-state levels of functional Jiv90 produced in Jiv90-overexpressing MDBK DNAJC14-KO Jiv90(WT) rescue cells did not result in complete NS2-3 cleavage in either NCP7- or CP7-infected MDBK DNAJC14-KO Jiv90(WT) rescue cells (
Fig. 6B
), which is also in agreement with earlier observations (
15
).", "location": "results", "reason": "This study is a replication that generates new CRISPR/Cas9 DNAJC14-knockout and GST-Jiv90 rescue cell lines to re-test and extend previously published findings on DNAJC14 dependency. Both the shipped record and blind target 1 identify Reference 15 (Lackner et al., 2005, doi: 10.1128/jvi.79.15.9746-9755.2005) as the replicated original study, which established that cellular DNAJC14 (Jiv/Jiv90) determines BVDV-1 NS2-3 cleavage regulation, noncp RNA replication, and persistence, and supplied the GST-Jiv90(WT) and GST-Jiv90(W39A) constructs re-tested in Fig. 5 and Fig. 6. In contrast, the other two references flagged by the blind coder are not separate replication targets: Reference 14 (Lackner et al., 2004, target 0) is an earlier study in the same chain cited as background for baseline NCP7 vs. CP7 replication differences in naive MDBK control cells, and Reference 53 (Lackner et al., 2006, target 2) is cited in the Introduction and Discussion for mechanistic interpretation of the Reference 15 GST-Jiv90 rescue experiment regarding NS2 binding domains and J-domain independence.", "method": "argon"}, "outcome": {"quote": "The functional importance of DNAJC14 for noncp pestiviruses has been established so far only for BVDV-1. It was therefore enigmatic whether replication of other noncp pestiviruses is also DNAJC14 dependent. By generating bovine and porcine DNAJC14 knockout cells, we could show that (i) replication of 6 distinct noncp pestivirus species (A to D, F, and G) depends on DNAJC14, (ii) the pestiviral replicase NS3-5B can assemble into functional complexes in the absence of DNAJC14, and (iii) all cp pestiviruses replicate their RNA and generate infectious progeny independent of host DNAJC14. Together, these findings confirm DNAJC14 as a pivotal cellular cofactor for the replication and maintenance of the noncp biotype of pestiviruses.", "location": "abstract", "reason": "Both the shipped record and blind target 1 code the replication outcome for Lackner et al. (2005) (Reference 15) as successful. Using newly generated CRISPR/Cas9 DNAJC14-knockout and GST-Jiv90(WT)/GST-Jiv90(W39A) rescue cell lines, the authors confirm both overall and in direct experimental comparisons to Reference 15 that noncp BVDV-1 (and five additional pestivirus species) require functional DNAJC14/Jiv90 for regulated NS2-3 cleavage and viral RNA replication, whereas cp pestiviruses replicate independently of endogenous DNAJC14, and that GST-Jiv90(WT) rescue restores NCP7 replication and deregulates NS2-3 cleavage without complete cleavage, in agreement with Reference 15.", "method": "argon"}}

{"outcome": {"quote": "One main finding of this paper is that, while we were unable to detect as strong a primacy effect 

as Asch (1946) originally reported, on balance there is evidence for a primacy effect in 

impression formation.", "location": {"start_line": 1247, "end_line": 1251, "form_feed_page": 18}, "reason": "The paper aimed both to replicate Asch's (1946, Exp. VI) primacy effect in impression formation and to test a novel extension regarding a pragmatic explanation (human vs. random computer speaker). Under the codebook (Step 3, Rule 1 and Coding Rules), where a paper reports multiple studies testing the original finding, they are aggregated following the author's explicit overall verdict, and a smaller effect size than the original still codes as 'successful' when the author treats the finding as supported on balance. The author explicitly concludes that 'on balance there is evidence for a primacy effect in impression formation' and 'In conclusion, we found evidence of a primacy effect in impression formation, although one that is substantially smaller than originally reported by Asch (1946).' The shipped 'mixed' coding improperly conflated the successful replication of Asch's primacy effect with the null result for the novel pragmatic-account extension.", "method": "argon"}}

{"type": {"quote": "A previous genome-wide association study (GWAS) suggested a strong association between the single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) rs10510181 in the proximity of the gene encoding a cell adhesion molecule with homology to L1CAM (CHL1) and adolescent idiopathic scoliosis (AIS) in Caucasians. To clarify the role of
CHL1
in the etiopathogenesis of AIS, we performed a case-control replication study in a Han Chinese population.", "location": "Abstract, Background (lines 34-36)", "reason": "The study collected new genetic data from an independent sample of 500 female Han Chinese AIS patients and 500 age- and sex-matched controls to re-test the published association between SNP rs10510181 near CHL1 and AIS reported by Sharma et al. (2011), making it a replication.", "method": "argon"}, "original": {"quote": "Recently, Sharma et al. [
15
] reported the association between SNP rs10510181, in the proximity of the CHL1 gene, and AIS by performing GWAS in 419 Caucasian families with AIS. The present study was conducted to validate the association of SNP rs10510181 with AIS.", "location": "Discussion, paragraph 2 (lines 174-176); Reference 15 (line 262)", "reason": "Both the shipped record and blind coding (target 0) identify Sharma et al. (2011) [15] (DOI: 10.1093/hmg/ddq571) as the tested original study. The primary text explicitly states that the present study was conducted to validate the association of SNP rs10510181 with adolescent idiopathic scoliosis (AIS) reported by Sharma et al. [15]. Other cited genetic studies (e.g., references 6-14 for other loci and reference 18 for CHL1 tag SNP intervals in schizophrenia) are cited solely as background or for tag SNP selection, not re-tested.", "method": "argon"}, "outcome": {"quote": "Recently, Sharma et al. [
15
] reported the association between SNP rs10510181, in the proximity of the CHL1 gene, and AIS by performing GWAS in 419 Caucasian families with AIS. The present study was conducted to validate the association of SNP rs10510181 with AIS. The results showed that rs10510181 was not associated with AIS in a Han Chinese population. Furthermore, 4 SNPs covering the entire region of the CHL1 gene were not associated with the occurrence of AIS either, indicating that the CHL1 gene might not be associated with AIS.
The previously reported association of rs10510181 with AIS predisposition in Caucasians was not replicated in our study.", "location": "Discussion, paragraph 2-3 (lines 174-177)", "reason": "Although initial Stage I screening (186 cases, 169 controls) showed a putative allelic difference for rs10510181, enlarging the sample in Stage II to the full powered cohort (500 cases, 500 controls) showed no significant genotype (P = 0.39) or allelic (P = 0.30) association, nor any association across four additional CHL1 SNPs. Following Codebook Step 3 Rule 1, the authors state an explicit overall failure verdict: 'The previously reported association of rs10510181 with AIS predisposition in Caucasians was not replicated in our study.'", "method": "argon"}}

{"type": {"quote": "Using a sample of 2,509 probands with ASD from the Simons Simplex Collection (SSC), this study provides the first replication of the ADOS domain CSS in an independent sample. Consistent with the original standardization study, when applied to existing SSC data, the ADOS domain CSS were less influenced by age and cognitive ability compared to raw domain totals.", "location": "Scientific Abstract, line 50", "reason": "The study evaluates the published ADOS domain CSS calibration findings of Hus, Gotham, & Lord (2014) in an independent sample of 2,509 children from the Simons Simplex Collection rather than re-analyzing the original calibration dataset. Both shipped and blind correctly classify the record type as replication.", "method": "argon"}, "original": {"quote": "The ADOS domain calibrated severity scores were created to provide separate estimates of Social Affect (SA-CSS) and Restricted, Repetitive Behaviors (RRB-CSS) that are relatively independent of child characteristics (
Hus et al., 2014
). Using a sample of 2,509 probands with ASD from the Simons Simplex Collection (SSC), this study provides the first replication of the ADOS domain CSS in an independent sample.", "location": "Scientific Abstract, lines 48-50 (and References, line 815)", "reason": "The paper explicitly replicates the ADOS domain CSS calibration study for Modules 1-3 by Hus, Gotham, & Lord (2014) (DOI: 10.1007/s10803-012-1719-1, listed in References at line 815), testing 8 of its 12 age/language cells in the SSC sample. The shipped row mispaired this citation with a same-year sibling paper on ADOS Module 4 by Hus & Lord (2014) (DOI: 10.1007/s10803-014-2080-3), which is mentioned only once in passing in the Discussion (line 668) as background context and is not tested.", "method": "argon"}, "outcome": {"quote": "This replication study confirms earlier independent findings (
Hus et al., 2014
) that the ADOS calibrated domain scores effectively reduced associations with child characteristics compared to raw domain totals in the Simon’s Simplex Collection (SSC). Twenty-seven percent of the variance in SA-Raw totals was explained by non-ASD child characteristics; standardization of scores reduced relationships to 3.1% for SA-CSS. Similarly, associations were reduced from 22.8% of variance in RRB-Raw to 4.5% of RRB-CSS; Verbal IQ emerged as the strongest predictor, explaining only 2–3% of variance of either domain calibrated score.", "location": "Discussion, lines 640-642", "reason": "After resolving the dependent original identity for row 412 to Hus, Gotham, & Lord (2014) (10.1007/s10803-012-1719-1, blind target_index 0), the authors state an explicit overall replication verdict confirming that the ADOS domain calibrated severity scores effectively reduced associations with non-ASD child characteristics (age and cognitive/developmental level) compared to raw domain totals in the independent SSC sample. Both shipped and blind correctly code the outcome as successful.", "method": "argon"}}

{"type": {"quote": "We replicate and expand a recent study by Simonovits, Guess,

and Nagler (2019) to explore if its conclusion – a pronounced and uniform conservative

