${d}^{\prime }$\end{document}  from signal detection theory (see Results section). In their experiments, Soto et al. did not find a significant correlation ( r  = −.18,  p  = .41), but we did ( r  = .228,  p  < .001). The reason why we did not reproduce Soto et al.’s null correlation is possibly due to their comparatively lower power (only 22 participants) which did not allow them to detect the subtle but significant correlation.", "location": "Discussion > Preregistered inferential analyses (lines 377-397)", "reason": "This multisite Registered Report re-tests three statistical findings from Soto et al. (2011): (1) above-chance working memory (WM) performance on unseen cue-present trials, (2) a non-significant correlation between cue detection sensitivity and WM performance, and (3) an above-chance regression intercept of WM performance on sensitivity. While the authors replicate the above-chance WM performance in unseen trials (Hypothesis 1) and the positive regression intercept (Hypothesis 3), they explicitly state that they did not reproduce Soto et al.'s null correlation (Hypothesis 2), finding a significant positive correlation instead (r = .228, p < .001 vs. Soto et al.'s r = -.18, p = .41). Under the codebook, where the authors confirm the primary tested effect as one comparison but explicitly mark another tested finding as differing from the original without a superseding whole-replication verdict, the replication outcome is 'mixed'. Both shipped and blind codings correctly recorded 'mixed' (though the prior blind quote omitted intervening text between lines 377 and 397, which is preserved contiguously here).", "method": "argon"}}

{"type": {"quote": "In 2015, as part of the Prostate Cancer Foundation–Movember Foundation Reproducibility Initiative, we published a Registered Report (
Shan et al., 2015
) that described how we intended to replicate selected experiments from the paper “Androgen Receptor Splice Variants Determine Taxane Sensitivity in Prostate Cancer” (
Thadani-Mulero et al., 2014
). Here we report the results of those experiments.", "location": "abstract, lines 94-98", "reason": "The study collected new experimental data (mouse xenograft tumor growth assays and Western blots) to re-test previously published findings from Thadani-Mulero et al. (2014), which is a replication under the codebook.", "method": "argon"}, "original": {"quote": "In 2015, as part of the Prostate Cancer Foundation–Movember Foundation Reproducibility Initiative, we published a Registered Report (
Shan et al., 2015
) that described how we intended to replicate selected experiments from the paper “Androgen Receptor Splice Variants Determine Taxane Sensitivity in Prostate Cancer” (
Thadani-Mulero et al., 2014
). Here we report the results of those experiments.", "location": "abstract, lines 94-98", "reason": "The shipped row cited the replication team's own Stage 1 Registered Report protocol (Shan et al., 2015, 10.7717/peerj.1232) rather than the original study whose findings were re-tested (Thadani-Mulero et al., 2014, 10.1158/0008-5472.can-13-2876). Per the codebook, a registered-report protocol is ruled out in favor of the paper reporting the tested original finding.", "method": "argon"}, "outcome": {"quote": "Additionally, we were unable to see any effect from the docetaxel treatment from either cell line, which may have been due to this difference in growth rate between the two tumor lines, or potentially to a compromised quality of the docetaxel reagent. While a future study could include a positive quality control for the reagent, it is outside the scope of this replication study as it was not factored into the plan at the stage of the Registered Report. It was unexpected that there would be such a lack of response to the docetaxel treatment and thus a positive quality control test was not included in the Registered Report and thus not replicated here. These factors contribute to a strongly inconclusive replication attempt of the original paper as we are unable to demonstrate that the ARv7 expression resulted in the difference in docetaxel treatment.", "location": "Results and Discussion, line 330", "reason": "Evaluated against the resolved tested original (Thadani-Mulero et al., 2014). Although the shipped coding relied on an individual comparison for LuCaP 86.2 tumor growth, the authors explicitly state an overall verdict in the Discussion that the attempt is 'not a strong test of the original hypothesis' and is a 'strongly inconclusive replication attempt of the original paper' due to undetectable ARv7 in LuCaP 23.1, slow tumor growth, N20 antibody failure, and lack of a positive quality control for the docetaxel reagent. Under Codebook Step 3 Rule 1, this special overall verdict governs and maps to 'uninformative'.", "method": "argon"}}

{"type": {"quote": "The study’s initial population of more than 300 million tweet IDs processed in a data collection pipeline is not publicly available. However, the replication archive allows access to all tweet IDs classified by the article’s deep-learning algorithm as containing violent content.
